the major motivation in this opposition  is is kind of a turf war  daelin Dorsey runs an OBGYN Clinic in  Scottsdale Arizona like many doctors she  runs her own independent practice with a  regular client base and a full-time  staff that works under her supervision  but daily endorsee is not a doctor she's  a nurse practitioner I decided to open  up my own practice because I really  wanted to have control over the way that  I provided care I wanted to provide  advanced nursing and working under the  auspices of the physician you're  providing care according to their  guidelines the the medical model and not  so much the nursing model which is a bit  different Arizona is one of 18 states  that allows nurse practitioners to have  their own medical practices they can  treat patients make diagnoses even  prescribe medication all without  oversight from a doctor that big thing  that nurses provide is we're easier to  be educated we can come to the workforce  a lot faster nurse practitioners are  proven to be the quality and cost  effective providers of health care nurse  practitioners across the country are  pushing for the freedom afforded in  Arizona saying it will provide more  low-cost affordable health care at a  time when more people than ever need it  but in some states doctors are pushing  back just as hard their training is very  limited compared to physicians I have by  the time I finished my pediatric  residency Oh 24,000 hours of training  experience that's multiples of what  nurse practitioners have California's  nurse practitioners made a push to  legalize independent practices but were  stopped dead in their tracks thanks in  part to the powerful California Medical  Association SB 491 was eventually killed  in committee and that was basically at  the behest of the California Medical  Association which is the organization  that represents physicians in the state  and the main reason for that is they  wanted to prevent competition from the  nurse practitioners they're not  physicians they lack a certain kind of  experience that I believe is very  important to the safety of patients and  the quality of medical  care that we're providing its true nurse  practitioners do not have the same level  of medical education that a physician  does but I think physicians are probably  a bit over educated for most of what  they do you don't have to have all that  education to provide the most basic of  primary care and that is where nurses  just fit the bill perfectly it would be  risky to assume that just opening the  floodgates to independent practice would  not be without some substantial downside  now I suppose you could do a test and  just see what happens I think that doing  so would put patients at risk luckily we  have done a sort of test in the eighteen  states which already have legalized  independent practice for nurse  practitioners physician groups point to  maps like this and say that nurses  haven't filled in the gaps in  underserved rural areas as promised but  even if that's true a Health Affairs  Survey of 26 published studies showed  that there's no evidence that the  primary care nurse practitioners provide  is in any way inferior or more expensive  than that of physicians in fact patients  actually reported a higher level of  satisfaction with nurse practitioners  there's no studies that show what you're  saying in terms of decreased quality of  care coming from nurse practitioners if  you have if you have an issue a health  issue and you don't go to a mechanic you  have always gone to your physician  there's no evidence that had you gone to  a mechanic there would be a downside on  the other hand that would be a really  bad idea our culture says the most  highly educated person is the only  person that's able to make a diagnosis  and offer a treatment plan but we know  based on the evidence and the literature  research studies prove that that's not  truly the case the licensing laws are  almost always sold as being in the  public interest it's going to protect  public health and safety but in reality  all they do is drive up prices and  reduce competition which reduces the  incentive to provide good services to  the consumer nourish pect  are making physicians a lot of money and  of course they want to keep us under  their control we don't think we're  trying to take over your job we want to  work in conjunction with you and that's  the only way I think that our healthcare  system is going to improve is if  everybody starts working together and  working to the fullest of what they're  capable and able and allowed by  legislation to do
 

